# Drafted by Gemini (R19 folk), edited. Tobias Dibbs, Hester Bolton, the grammar headstone, the Epitaph Competition and Names on Stones.

~ dibbs
Narrator: Behind the parlour counter, a rosy-cheeked lad in a canvas apron whets an iron spade with a stone as briskly as if packing for a summer picnic.
tobias_dibbs [smile]: Tobias Dibbs at your service, apprentice in earth and ash. It is a rare treat to meet travellers who know the business end of a good shovel.
* What do you do here? -> dibbs_job
* [if not flag.mm_epitaph_asked] Is there anything we can help with? -> dibbs_contest
* Goodbye -> END

~ dibbs_job
tobias_dibbs [neutral]: Mostly I drain the bloat, wire the jaws shut so they look polite, and sweep the teeth out of the grate. You work up a grand appetite for mutton pie by noon.
interject name:cordelia_skein: Wiring the jaw before you study the humours? What a waste of a fine corpse.
-> dibbs

~ dibbs_contest
set mm_epitaph_asked
quest epitaph_competition asked
tobias_dibbs [smile]: The folk here write their own epitaphs before they stiffen, and most are far too honest for church. I propped the best three on the bricks in the Cremation Gardens. Go pick me a champion.
interject name:delphine_ravelle: A graveyard variety show with top billing in brick, darlings; I would never miss the curtain.
-> END

~ epitaph_plaque_a
set mm_epitaph_a_read
Narrator: Scratched into a soot-stained brick is a neat, blunt rhyme: I TOLD THE APOTHECARY THOSE MUSHROOMS WERE SPOTTED, NOW HE IS PAID AND I AM ROTTED.
-> END

~ epitaph_plaque_b
set mm_epitaph_b_read
Narrator: A crooked slate plaque beside a clump of nettles reads: BURY ME FACE DOWN SO BARNABY NEXT DOOR CAN KISS WHAT IS LEFT.
-> END

~ epitaph_plaque_c
set mm_epitaph_c_read
Narrator: Chiseled deep into a cracked sandstone slab is a belated confession: THE SILVERGRAVE FAMILY SPOONS ARE UNDER THE THIRD FLOORBOARD BY THE CHIMNEY, SORRY MOTHER.
-> END

~ epitaph_judge
Narrator: Tobias bounces on his heels by the dry stone fountain, twirling a faded blue prize ribbon and a stub of carpenter's pencil.
* The one that complained about the neighbours -> epitaph_win_b
* The blunt one -> epitaph_win_a
* The honest confession -> epitaph_win_c
* [if flag.mm_epitaph_a_read or flag.mm_epitaph_b_read or flag.mm_epitaph_c_read] A tie, honestly -> epitaph_tie
* [ends:epitaph_competition] They are all too honest. Call it off -> epitaph_off

~ epitaph_win_a
approve tobin_hayward +2 cordelia_skein +2: You gave the epitaph ribbon to the blunt warning about spotted mushrooms
quest epitaph_competition done
set mm_epitaph_winner = 1
gold +25
tobias_dibbs [smile]: The mushroom man takes the blue ribbon, short and sweet as arsenic. Listen to the ashes rustling in the urns, they always love a punchline.
-> END

~ epitaph_win_b
approve delphine_ravelle +2 silas_vane +2 rook_coldwake -1: You rewarded a spiteful tombstone jab at the neighbour next door
quest epitaph_competition done
set mm_epitaph_winner = 2
gold +25
tobias_dibbs [smile]: A prize for neighbourly spite, and well earned too. Old Barnaby in plot four is going to rattle his lid off when he hears.
-> END

~ epitaph_win_c
approve rook_coldwake +2 silas_vane -1: You chose the honest confession about the stolen family spoons
quest epitaph_competition done
set mm_epitaph_winner = 3
gold +25
tobias_dibbs [smile]: Nothing warms the Cremation Gardens like a deathbed confession about stolen silver. I will chisel that in deep before his mother digs herself up to check.
-> END

~ epitaph_tie
quest epitaph_competition done
set mm_epitaph_winner = 4
tobias_dibbs [neutral]: A three-way split means nobody sulks and everybody gets a snippet of ribbon. Best fetch my chisel and three fresh bricks from the shed.
-> END

~ epitaph_off
approve delphine_ravelle -2 silas_vane -2 rook_coldwake +1: You called off the cremation garden epitaph contest for being too honest
quest epitaph_competition lost
tobias_dibbs [sad]: Well, I will fold the ribbon away, though the urns take a snub terribly hard. By cockcrow you will find they have scraped their own letters right off the brick.
-> END

~ hester
Narrator: Hester Bolton plants her boots on the wet cobbles of the square, her raw red forearms folded tight beneath a lifted chin.
hester_bolton [angry]: My useless husband Gregor has gone and turned himself into a flesh-eating ghast, and the first of the month is tomorrow. Dying twice does not excuse a man from owing five gold to the landlord.
* We are sorry for your loss -> hester_loss
* [if gold >= 5] Pay the rent for her -> hester_rent
* Where is Gregor now? -> hester_where
* Goodbye -> END

~ hester_loss
hester_bolton [weary]: The man himself I can forgive, he was cold in the feet anyway. It is the six silver crowns for the cellar roof that breaks my heart.
interject name:silas_vane: Dying twice to dodge a five-gold tab is a bold play, friend, even by dockside rules.
-> hester

~ hester_rent
approve tobin_hayward +3 rook_coldwake +2 silas_vane -1: You paid Hester Bolton's rent out of your own purse
gold -5
hester_bolton [smile]: Six honest crowns, warm in the palm and counted twice. When I see Gregor shuffling by the fence, I will tell him a stranger had better manners.
set mm_hester_paid
-> END

~ hester_where
hester_bolton [neutral]: Look for Gregor wherever the ghasts are thickest and doing the least work. If the Mayor had mended our leaky slate roof, the damp would never have taken him.
interject name:delphine_ravelle: Even among the risen dead, darlings, there is always someone coasting in the chorus line.
-> hester

~ grammar_stone
Narrator: Beside the rusted iron gate leans a narrow granite headstone engraved in a fussy, pinched hand: HERE LYES A PEDANT, WHO CORRECTS YOU. Below the moss, someone has scratched a comma out and carved it back in deeper.
* Read the epitaph aloud -> grammar_read
* Misspell something on purpose -> grammar_correct
* Leave -> END

~ grammar_read
Narrator: A dry, papery cough rises from the lichen on the granite slab, and a disembodied voice informs you that lyes is archaic, not an error, and that your vowels are slovenly.
-> END

~ grammar_correct
approve silas_vane +2 delphine_ravelle +2 lucan_houndstooth -2: You misspelled a word on purpose to bait a pedantic headstone
Narrator: The mossy headstone exhales a long sigh through its chiseled letters, and the crooked spelling shifts in the cold air until it reads true.
-> END

~ names_start
set mm_names_asked
quest names_on_stones asked
mori_shade [neutral]: Three graves in the Potter's Field still stand with bare cedar posts. My ledger holds the names of everyone our village lost to the mist. Match the tokens left beside them, and we can carve their proper stones.
-> END

~ names_graves
Narrator: Three unpainted cedar posts stand in a neat row through the wet grass, each with a small keepsake resting in the mud at its foot.
* [Investigation DC 12] Study the first token -> names_a | names_a_plain
* [Medicine DC 12] Study the second token -> names_b | names_b_plain
* [History DC 12] Study the third token -> names_c | names_c_plain
* Ask Mori to place them by the ledger -> names_mori
* Leave it -> END

~ names_a
set mm_names_a
Narrator: The first token is a singed corner of a linen baker's apron, still dusted with flour from the hearth of Holly Aster's mother.
-> names_graves

~ names_a_plain
Narrator: The first token is only a frayed scrap of gray linen, soaked with rain and bearing no mark to show whose hand once held it.
-> names_graves

~ names_b
set mm_names_b
Narrator: The second token is a carved wooden horse with one ear chipped away, the sort of pocket toy Old Rusty still remembers a Lockwood child carrying.
-> names_graves

~ names_b_plain
Narrator: The second token is a small wooden toy worn smooth by rainwater and thumbs, telling you nothing at all of the child who dropped it.
-> names_graves

~ names_c
set mm_names_c
Narrator: The third token is a tarnished brass thimble stamped with a tiny shears mark, left in memory of someone dear that Thomasin Ardor lost.
-> names_graves

~ names_c_plain
Narrator: The third token is a dented brass thimble crusted with dark peat, its rim too worn for your eyes to read any maker's stamp.
-> names_graves

~ names_mori
mori_shade [neutral]: My ledger matches every one of these small tokens to a name in faded ink. You ought to carry word to their kin in person before I take up the chisel.
set mm_names_a
set mm_names_b
set mm_names_c
-> names_graves

~ names_tell_holly
if flag.mm_names_a
set mm_names_told_a
Narrator: Holly Aster sets her warm tin tray upon the counter, wipes her floury palms, and speaks her mother's name aloud for the first time in years.
endif
-> END

~ names_tell_rusty
if flag.mm_names_b
set mm_names_told_b
Narrator: Old Rusty leans on his birch broom in the lane and murmurs a child's name that nobody on the hill has spoken since the fog took them.
endif
-> END

~ names_tell_thomasin
if flag.mm_names_c
set mm_names_told_c
Narrator: Thomasin Ardor closes both calloused hands around the cold brass thimble and stares at her worktable without speaking a word for a long while.
endif
-> END

~ names_done
if flag.mm_names_told_a and flag.mm_names_told_b and flag.mm_names_told_c
quest names_on_stones done
mori_shade [smile]: Those three graves bear their true names in clean slate at last. Even the damp earth of the Potter's Field will remember who rests under the grass.
interject name:rook_coldwake: Proper slate, clean lettering, all squared away in the shift log.
else
mori_shade [neutral]: The names are inked beside my chisel, though some among the bereaved down in Wickermoor still wait to hear the news from your lips.
endif
-> END

~ names_drop
quest names_on_stones lost
mori_shade [sad]: The three cedar posts will stay blank against the rain a while longer. Without a spoken name to anchor it, this field will simply keep forgetting its dead.
-> END
